SEC. 30001. FUNDING CAP FOR THE BUREAU OF CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION.

This section amends the Consumer Financial Protection Act of 2010 to reduce the annual funding cap for the Bureau of Consumer Financial Protection from 12% to 6.5% of the total payments made by the Federal Reserve System to the Bureau. The change is made by striking "12" and inserting "6.5" in the specified statutory provision (12 U.S.C. 5497(a)(2)(A)(iii)). This effectively halves the maximum amount the Bureau can receive from the Federal Reserve, limiting its operational budget. The amendment takes effect upon enactment of this Act.

SEC. 30002. RESCISSION OF FUNDS FOR GREEN AND RESILIENT RETROFIT PROGRAM FOR MULTIFAMILY HOUSING.

This section rescinds all unobligated balances of amounts previously made available under section 30002(a) of Public Law 117-169 (the Inflation Reduction Act, enacted August 16, 2022) for the Green and Resilient Retrofit Program for Multifamily Housing. The rescission applies to funds that were not yet obligated as of the date of enactment of this Act. This action eliminates the remaining funding for that program, which was intended to support energy efficiency and resilience improvements in multifamily housing. No new appropriations are provided.

SEC. 30003. SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION RESERVE FUND.

This section eliminates the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Reserve Fund by repealing subsection (i) of Section 4 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 and redesignating subsections (j) and (k) as (i) and (j). It also amends Section 21F(g)(2) to clarify that the SEC's whistleblower fund is available without further appropriation or fiscal year limitation for paying awards. During the transition period from enactment to October 1, 2025, the SEC may spend previously obligated Reserve Fund amounts on ongoing programs. Effective October 1, 2025, all remaining obligated and unobligated balances in the Reserve Fund are transferred to the general fund of the Treasury, and the account is closed.

SEC. 30004. APPROPRIATIONS FOR DEFENSE PRODUCTION ACT.

This section appropriates $1,000,000,000 for fiscal year 2025, out of amounts not otherwise appropriated, to remain available until September 30, 2027, for carrying out the Defense Production Act (50 U.S.C. 4501 et seq.). These funds are in addition to any other amounts available for that purpose. The appropriation is intended to support the President's authority to prioritize and expand domestic industrial production for national defense. The funds are available for obligation and expenditure through the specified period. [end of text]


